There are two reasons why it isn't working.  First, the reason why no
variable is being set:    When you run a shell script, a new shell is
started up, with its own environment, and when that shell terminates,
its environment disappears, having no effect on the shell that ran it.
In other words, NETHACKOPTIONS was set in the environment of the shell
that you ran, but not in the shell that you ran it from.  The way to fix
that is, instead of simply running the shell script, _source_ it. E.g.
> source myhackoptionsscript
This tells the shell NOT to start up a new shell, but to take commands
from the file and interpret them in the current shell.  (This works for
csh and tcsh; I don't know about sh or ksh).

The second reason: there are for some reason two environments, one
set with "set", and one set with "setenv".  I think ones set with
setenv are the only ones that get sent to programs that you run from
the shell.  (Anyone care to enlighten us?)
In any case, you have to setenv the NETHACKOPTIONS variable.
E.g.:
setenv NETHACKOPTIONS "dogname:Fritz,notombstone"
Note the difference in syntax from "set": no '=' is expected.

You might want to just put the "setenv ..." command in your .cshrc file.
That would seem most convenient.  Then in every shell that got started
up the variable would already be set. (Again I am assuming csh or tcsh.)

As for escaped characters, the following require a \ before them:
! ? * " ' ` $ and of course \
These are the main ones; { might also require it. See man csh or whatever
your shell is.


